      "id": 1,
      "text": "Thompsonville takes its name from Orrin Thompson, who founded the town’s carpet industry in 1828.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.enfield-ct.gov/1249/Thompsonville-Revitalization",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s first mill, called White Mill, hired skilled weavers from Scotland to produce fine textiles.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thompsonville%2C_Connecticut",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Railroad planners laid tracks through Thompsonville, and a later gas boom attracted factories that reshaped the local economy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://hometownhistorymaps.com/pages/history-of-thompsonville-connecticut",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Historic Sanborn Fire Insurance Maps of Thompsonville, dating from 1885 to 1912, show the town’s original streets and businesses.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://hometownhistorymaps.com/pages/history-of-thompsonville-connecticut",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1988, a renovated factory complex opened, preserving the original buildings’ character while silencing industrial noise.",
